GRAPH THEORY  •  TYPES, VARIABLES & FUNDAMENTALS
→ 511.5 → Graph Theory

CORE OBJECT
  G = (V, E)
  V  vertices / nodes
  E  edges / links
  n = |V|   order
  m = |E|   size
  deg(v)    degree of vertex v
  N(v)      neighborhood of v

GRAPH TYPES
  Simple          no loops, no multiple edges
  Multigraph      multiple edges allowed
  Directed        edges have orientation (arcs)
  Weighted        edges carry costs / capacities
  Tree            connected, acyclic
  Forest          disjoint union of trees
  Network         directed + capacities (flows)
  Tournament      oriented complete graph
  Bipartite       V = A ∪ B, edges only between A and B
  Arborescence    directed tree rooted outward / inward

SEARCH
  BFS   visit nearest neighbors first     (layers / distance)
  DFS   walk down each branch first       (backtracking)

PATHFINDING
  Shortest Path            min-weight route between two nodes
  Single-Source SP         from one root to all others
  All-Pairs SP             every node to every other node
  Minimum Spanning Tree    cheapest tree connecting all nodes
  Random Walk              follow any edge at random
                           ("drunkard's walk")

CLASSIC RESULTS
  Eulerian circuit     exists ⇔ all degrees even (connected)
  Hamiltonian cycle    visits every vertex exactly once
  Hall's Marriage Thm  bipartite matching condition
  Menger's Theorem     min cut = max number of disjoint paths
  Matrix-Tree Theorem  number of spanning trees via Laplacian
  Turán's Theorem      extremal edges without K_r
  BEST Theorem         number of Eulerian circuits in digraphs
  Gallai–Milgram       path cover vs. independence number

NETWORK FLOWS
  capacity c(e), flow f(e) ≤ c(e)
  conservation at intermediate vertices
  max-flow min-cut

COLORING
  χ(G)  chromatic number
  proper coloring: adjacent vertices different colors

COMMON NOTATION
  G[S]     induced subgraph on S ⊆ V
  G - v    delete vertex
  G - e    delete edge
  κ(G)     connectivity
  λ(G)     edge-connectivity
  α(G)     independence number
  ω(G)     clique number
